$DIR/main.p:28:2: cannot pass &in to fmt.Printf, which keeps it after returning
    &in points into in. in is a local variable of f (declared at main.p:25).
    fmt.Printf passes a pointer reachable from args to Fprintf
    (print_linux.p:9), which passes a pointer reachable from args to
    (*printer).doPrintf (fmt.p:929), which passes a pointer reachable from args
    to (*printer).printArg (fmt.p:880), which passes a pointer reachable from
    arg to a formatting method of what it walks (which may call main.(E).String)
    (fmt.p:671), which stores e in package variable keep (main.p:19), so &in
    would outlive in.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If fmt.Printf should keep its own copy of in, change it to take the value,
    not a pointer, or an owner it can keep: an owned[Inner] from new, a
    shared[Inner], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:28:2: cannot pass arr to fmt.Printf, which keeps it after returning
    arr is an interface that points at a copy of its value in f's frame.
    Converting a value larger than one word to an interface puts a copy of the
    value in the frame of the function that converts it (here f, at main.p:28),
    and the interface points at that copy. fmt.Printf passes a pointer reachable
    from args to Fprintf (print_linux.p:9), which passes a pointer reachable
    from args to (*printer).doPrintf (fmt.p:929), which passes a pointer
    reachable from args to (*printer).printArg (fmt.p:880), which passes arg to
    a formatting method of what it walks (which may call main.(E).String)
    (fmt.p:671), which stores e in package variable keep (main.p:19), so the
    interface would outlive f's frame.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Change fmt.Printf to take a value or an owner, which it can keep: an
    owned[T] from new, a shared[T], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
